  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Friday)\nIssued at 110 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nLatest satellite imagery shows moisture and clouds streaming into\nthe area from the southwest, this gives hints as to what can be \nexpected for the rest of today and this weekend. An upper level \nhigh pressure looks to slide to the east, this is giving the CWA \nsouthwest flow which opens the area up to the possibility for rain\nand storms.\n\nSo for the rest of today, moisture streaming over the mountains in\nWyoming and the Black Hills will be a main source for any\nthunderstorm activity. Latest guidance has storms initiating \nlater in the afternoon, say around 2 to 4PM. And once they \ndevelop, they will track eastward. Severe thunderstorms today\ncan't be ruled out but the chances are low. The SPC has placed a \nmarginal risk (level 1 of 5) of severe weather over the entire area\ntoday and the latest machine learning severe weather models give \naround 5 to 15% chance of storm cells being severe. The HRRR \nsevere weather probability model puts an 8 to 16% chance of \nsevere storms near the northern Black Hills and in the SD plains \nin the 4 to 9PM window. \n\nFor tonight and the weekend, hi res models keep trying to hint at\nanother round of rain moving into NE WY during the overnight hours\nbut confidence is low.\n\nAn upper level trough is expected to swing over Montana and North\nDakota. This will bring continued chances for rain and storms to\nthe CWA. So for Saturday afternoon, another round of showers and \nstorms is likely to track northeast through NE WY and into western\nSD. And once again, those storms could be strong to severe with \nhail and wind being the main concerns. Sunday is looking like a \nsimilar set up with storms developing in WY and tracking into SD.\n\nAfternoon temps will be warmer with highs in the upper 80s to mid\n90s Saturday and Sunday. Overall, well above normal for late \nAugust.\n\nFor a quick glance at next week, lingering low end chances for \ndaily passing showers and storms last well into next week but the\noverall pattern is still on the dry side. Above normal temps will\ncontinue as well with highs in the 80s to 90s.",
